+### The voice and text helper (optional)
+
+Off by default. It does two things. It lets a person say their answers on the home page, or say or type them at `/talk`: Meta's Model API turns speech into words and picks form answers out of the words, and the person checks them in the ordinary form. With the helper on, the home page (`/`) is a voice page in place of the landing page: one big round microphone, the consent tick, and a link to the form and one to `/talk`. It is voice-first, not voice-only. It has no typing box and no language choice, since the person speaks the page's language (set from the bar at the top). It opens with one open question, shows what was found for the person to check, asks for whatever is missing one question at a time, and ends at the first form page with something unanswered. `/talk` is the longer page and is unchanged: it opens with one open question, then asks the twelve form questions one at a time, each read aloud in the page's language by the browser's own speech engine, and nothing goes into the form until the person has seen what was found, with their own words beside it, and said it is right. The question's voice is a voice on the device if there is one, and it starts by itself once the consent box is ticked; otherwise it is one the browser reaches over the network, which reads only when the person presses Listen and carries only the question's fixed sentence, never anything the person said (PROCESSOR_SCHEDULE.md §10.2). The name and the business name are not asked aloud; the person types them on the next page. And on `/advisory` it can write a plan: after the person ticks a box, Muse Spark words a short plan in their language around blanks, and the rules fill the blanks with their own figures. A reply with a digit or a link of its own, or a blank that is unknown or missing, is refused, asked for once more and then dropped, and the advice the rules wrote stays on the page whatever happens. The helper computes nothing. [docs/HACKATHON.md](docs/HACKATHON.md) says what it is for and how it was built, and [docs/PROCESSOR_SCHEDULE.md](docs/PROCESSOR_SCHEDULE.md) §10 says what it sends, keeps and logs, which a deployer needs before switching it on.
+
+It needs both switches, and the server one needs a key:
+
+| Where | Setting | |
+|---|---|---|
+| backend | `ARTHNITI_AI_ENABLED=true` and `MODEL_API_KEY` | environment only. The backend reads no `.env` file, and it refuses to start with the helper on and no key |
+| backend | `ARTHNITI_AI_MODEL`, `ARTHNITI_AI_ASR_MODEL` | `muse-spark-1.3` and `muse-voice-transcribe-1.0` unless set. A `-contributor` model is refused, because those may be trained on |
+| backend | `ARTHNITI_AI_HOURLY_LIMIT` | calls an hour in all, counted by this process. Default 100 |
+| backend | `ARTHNITI_AI_SPEND_CAP_USD`, `ARTHNITI_AI_SPEND_FILE` | the most the app will let itself bill in all (default 10, at most 100), and the file that keeps the running total (default `backend/.ai_spend`, gitignored) |
+| frontend build | `VITE_AI_HELPER=true` | makes `/` the voice home page, shows the `/talk` page and the plan card on `/advisory`. Any other value leaves `/` the landing page, `/talk` a 404 and hides the card |
+
+Every call is priced at its worst case against the cap *before* it is sent, and a call that would pass the cap is refused. That keeps this app's own bill inside a number you chose. It is not Meta's limit and it is not a guarantee: deleting the file resets the total, and each server process counts its own hourly limit. Check the balance on Meta's billing page as well.
+
+To try it with a real key, type the key so it is not echoed or kept in the shell history, run the smoke script first, then start the backend:
+
+```bash
+cd backend
+read -rs MODEL_API_KEY; export MODEL_API_KEY       # paste the key, press Enter. Nothing shows
+export ARTHNITI_AI_ENABLED=true
+../.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m app.smoke_meta      # 3 real calls (2 text, 1 of silence): up to about $0.045 against the cap
+../.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m app.smoke_meta --plan   # 2 real calls, a plan in English and one in Hindi: about $0.08 against the cap
+../.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
+```
+
+and in another terminal `cd frontend && VITE_AI_HELPER=true npm run dev`. The smoke script needs only the key and sets the helper switch itself. On 27 October 2026 both runs passed against the live service, and the second recorded $0.0797 against the cap for calls whose reported token counts come to about 2 cents at the listed prices (the cap books the worst case before each call, by design). The test suite clears `MODEL_API_KEY`, and a guard in `tests/conftest.py` fails any test that connects to anything but this machine (it covers blocking sockets, not asyncio on Windows).
