@@ -138,0 +175,3 @@ Six behaviours worth knowing before you click through:
+- **Read aloud.** Under the light on `/analysis` and `/advisory`, a Listen button reads out the light, the note under it and the page's main sentence and next step, in the page's language. It reads no figure: a sentence with a digit in it is left out. It uses the browser's own speech engine, so nothing is sent anywhere to do it, no model is involved, and it never starts by itself. The button appears only where the browser reports a voice installed on the device for the page's language. A voice the browser reaches over the network is never used, because the sentence would leave the device. (The questions the home page and `/talk` ask aloud are the one exception: they are the page's own fixed wording and carry nothing the person said, so a voice over the network may read them, but only when Listen is pressed; a voice on the device starts by itself once the consent box is ticked.) **On the owner's Windows laptop (Edge, 1 November 2026) 7 of the 328 voices Edge lists run on the device, and the button shows in English and in Hindi: David, Mark and Zira for en-US, Ravi and Heera for en-IN, and Hemant and Kalpana for hi-IN, which the owner added in Windows' speech settings that evening (before that 5 of 326 ran on the device, all English, and the button showed in English only; a reading on 29 October found 321 voices and none on the device, which is what the first of the two lists Edge announces at launch holds). The voices Edge labels Online (Natural), such as Neerja and Prabhat (English, India) and स्वरा and मधुर (Hindi), run over the network and are never used**; whether a phone browser has an on-device Hindi voice has not been checked. The browser gate drives a fake speech engine. A one-off probe, not kept in the repo, also drove the real Edge on 1 November: in English the button appeared on 20 of 20 reloads of each result page and one press read the four lines in the Ravi voice from start to end; in Hindi none appeared on `/analysis`, until the Hindi voices were added that evening, after which it appeared on 5 of 5 reloads and a press read the four lines in the Hemant voice from start to end. The probe first showed that the button never came back after a reload, because Edge announces its voice list before the page is listening; the page now reads the list again once it is. The voices on the device are Windows' older synthetic ones; the owner found the English one robotic, and said on 2 November that the Hindi one, Hemant, is clear but robotic and that this is fine (nothing is recorded about Kalpana, the other Hindi voice). The button's words and the note under it (the `ra.*` keys) are machine-written in the 7 non-English languages and unreviewed.
+- **The traffic light.** `/analysis`, `/advisory` and `/report` show a green, yellow or red light, drawn as a colour, a shape (circle, triangle, octagon) and a word, so it reads in print and to someone who cannot tell the colours apart. The backend picks it from what it has already worked out (`mvp_summary.band`): green when nothing is flagged (profitable, feasible, low repayment pressure, no money missing, the household within its limit), red when the plan makes no profit, the repayment pressure is high or the household is over its limit, yellow for everything else. It reads only the figures the reader typed, and the note under it says so: it is not a decision about a loan or a scheme, and green does not mean the reader qualifies for anything. Python only, no model. A result stored before the light existed keeps the earlier mark. The rule is the author's own and has not been checked by anyone else.
+- **Simple or Detailed.** `/analysis` and `/advisory` open with a two-button switch, "How much to show". Simple keeps the light, the verdict, the four headline figures and the next steps on `/analysis`, and the light, the main point, the action plan, the loan-offer card and (with the helper on) the written-plan card on `/advisory`. Detailed shows everything else too: the working, the market prices, the observations, the government support, the loan options, the scheme funding and the registrations. The page opens in Simple, because most of the people this is for read little. A reader who never presses Detailed therefore does not see the loan options, the scheme funding, the registrations or the local prices; the note under the switch says there is more. The choice is a display setting like the language and the theme: it is kept in `localStorage` (`sih-detail`) only once a button has been pressed, and never with the answers. The hidden sections are removed from the page, not folded, so a screen reader skips them too. `/schemes`, `/report` and the printout of `/report` are not affected.
@@ -139,0 +179,7 @@ Six behaviours worth knowing before you click through:
+- **A figure under the advice.** A sentence with a digit in it is left out of the read-aloud and of the written plan, so the advice stays as it was written and its figure is printed in a line under it: under the next step on `/analysis`, and under each recommendation on `/analysis`, `/advisory` and `/report` (those two show the first five). There are four kinds. *Spending*: how much more goes out each month than comes in, rounded up to the rupee. *Loan payments*: the most the payments on the loans the reader already has can be for the repayment pressure to read "low" (under 30%), and how much less than now that is; when the estimated loan for the money still needed is counted in, the line says so, because the limit is then lower. *Household*: the same for the household check, to keep the repayments within half of the household's income. *Cash reserve*: how much more the money in hand needs to reach the ₹10,000 the advice suggests keeping. A limit is found by asking the function that prints the pressure figure, over whole rupees, so the line never offers a limit that the page then contradicts, and an amount is rounded the way that makes doing what it says enough. A sentence gets no line where the rules have no honest number: no spending to cut, no household income given, or a limit that cutting the current payments cannot reach. The advice about the size of a new loan gets none either, because the loan options already give that number. Python only, no model, and the lines show in Simple as well. The Hindi of the 5 `amt.*` sentences was returned as final by the owner on 31 Oct 2026 (2 of the 5 changed); the other 6 non-English languages are machine-written and unreviewed.
+- **Loan options.** When the money in hand falls short of the investment, `/advisory` shows one card for each loan length (12, 24, 36 and 60 months). A card is the largest loan, in whole thousands of rupees and no bigger than the shortfall, whose repayment still counts as low pressure (under 30% of the monthly surplus) and keeps the household check within half of its income, at the same assumed 12% a year the risk figure uses. The rules mark the shortest length that covers the whole shortfall, and a card that does not cover it says how much is still missing. When no loan is safe, the page says why (no surplus, a household already over its limit, or no room left after the current loans). These are planning figures and not a quote: no lender has offered these terms, and nothing on the page says one will. Each card's total is the sum of its schedule, to the paisa; `/report` prints that schedule, one row a month (the payment, the interest, the part that repays the loan, what is still owed), with a menu to pick the length. The last payment differs a little from the rest, because it clears what the whole-rupee payments leave over. Beside it one line says how many times the money left each month covers the monthly loan payments, the loans already being paid included. Banks call that DSCR and may work it out differently. It is the repayment-pressure figure read the other way up, so it adds no new test of the plan, and no lender's cut-off is attached to it.
+- **Checking a loan offer.** Near the bottom of `/advisory`, once there is a result, a card lets the reader type in an offer a lender has made and see what it costs. They first say how it was told to them, with nothing chosen for them. *As payments*: the money they get in hand (after any fee is taken), each payment, how often (every day, week or month) and how many payments in all. The card finds the rate for one payment at which those payments are worth the money received, and multiplies it by the payments in a year, so interest is not added on interest; for payments every day it counts 365 a year and says the true cost is lower if the lender does not collect every day. *As a rate*: the rupees charged for every 100 rupees each month (say 3) and the months; the card counts that on the whole amount for all the months, with the whole amount paid back at the end, and the yearly cost is the monthly rate times 12. This way, a fee taken out first is not counted. Either way it prints what they get, what they pay back in all, what that adds and the yearly cost, then the same money over the same time at the rate the app assumes for its loan figures (12% a year), and, when the app priced a loan for the reader, that loan beside it. The comparison says it is not a quote from any lender, and the card does not claim that its yearly figure matches any lender's or regulator's APR. It is plain JavaScript in the browser (`frontend/src/data/loanOffer.js`): what is typed is not sent anywhere and is not written to `sessionStorage`, `localStorage` or a cookie, `tools/verify/offer-ui.mjs` watches the page's requests and storage to check it, and the card is left out of the printout. A box refuses a comma, a sign, a letter, zero or a number above its limit with a sentence saying so, and does not guess what was meant. The card never calls an offer illegal, too high or unfair and never says to take it or leave it; a word list checked in the tests guards that, which makes it a guard against a slip and not a proof. The Hindi of the 35 `offer.*` sentences was returned as final by the owner on 31 Oct 2026 (5 of the 35 changed); the other 6 non-English languages are machine-written and unreviewed.
+- **What the schemes put in and pay.** For PMEGP, PMFME and Mudra, when the matcher finds them, `/advisory` and `/report` show a card of what the reader would put in, what the scheme adds and what is left to borrow, worked out on the money the reader said they need to start. PMEGP is shown twice, once for the general category and once for a special one (women, SC, ST, OBC, minorities and others its guidelines name), because the app never asks which the reader is and the card leaves them to read their own. PMEGP counts at most ₹20 lakh of the cost, and PMFME's government money stops at ₹10 lakh a unit. Mudra has no subsidy and no stated own share, so the card names the loan size the money still needed falls in. These are sums on the terms each scheme has published, with a caveat for each (see the limits at the end). The card checks no eligibility, does not test whether the repayment fits the household, and says its figures are estimates and not an offer. Python only, no model.
+- **Registrations to check.** `/advisory` and `/report` list four things to ask about for the reader's kind of work: Udyam registration (free, done online), the local office (the Gram Panchayat in a village, the municipal or town office elsewhere), FSSAI for a food business, and GST. Each step is marked "Usually needed", "Depends on your business", "Probably not needed", "Free to register" or "Ask the office", and names the published source it comes from with the date it was read: the FSSAI licensing rules (as compiled on 9 November 2017), the CBIC booklet on GST (1 June 2019) and the Udyam portal (28 October 2026). The limits are tested against the reader's expected monthly income times 12, and the card says so. It decides nothing, it never says a registration is required, and it ends by telling the reader to confirm with the office concerned. The limits are constants in `compliance_service.py`; which kinds of work count as food, goods or services is our reading, and the card does not hide that.
+- **Check these first.** On `/schemes` and `/report`, each scheme opens with the few of its own eligibility lines that decide whether it fits a person: who may apply, what kind of unit it covers, whether it is running at all. 93 of the 99 schemes have such lines (212 in all); the six that name nobody in particular have none. The lines sit above the closed list of eligibility rules, so the reader sees them before the trip, and the text under the heading says "This scheme might not fit you if one of these is not true for you. We cannot check them for you." That is the truth: the form asks for no age, sex, community, trade or membership, so no eligibility rule is evaluated anywhere, and the lines are plain bullets, never ticks. They are picked by hand in `backend/gen_schemes.py` (`CHECK_FIRST`), Python only, no model. The heading and the sentence under it (`sc.checkFirst`, `sc.checkFirstText`) are machine-written in the 7 non-English languages and unreviewed.
